::ENG1_U01_L06_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG1.V.01] Passage: The article gives three details that prove the school garden saved water. Question: Which sentence uses academic vocabulary correctly?{
=A. The article uses evidence to show the garden saved water.#Correct. Evidence is the academic word for details that prove or support an idea.
~B. The article uses stuff to be good.#Incorrect. Stuff and good are vague words that do not clearly explain the idea.
~C. The article evidences the garden with water things.#Incorrect. Evidence is not used correctly in this sentence.
~D. The garden was academic because water happened.#Incorrect. Academic does not fit the meaning of the passage.
}

::ENG1_U01_L06_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG1.V.01] Passage: One response says, "The sign helped." Another says, "The sign clarified which hallway was open." Question: Which word best explains why the second response is stronger?{
~A. vague#Incorrect. The second response is not vague; it is more exact.
~B. unrelated#Incorrect. The second response is related to the sign.
=C. precise#Correct. Precise means exact and clear, which describes the second response.
~D. hidden#Incorrect. The meaning is clearer, not hidden.
}

::ENG1_U01_L06_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG1.V.01] Passage: The student explained aloud that the two texts had different moods. Question: Which oral explanation uses academic vocabulary correctly?{
~A. "The texts were different and stuff."#Incorrect. This is too vague for an academic oral explanation.
=B. "The two texts contrast in mood because one feels hopeful and the other feels serious."#Correct. Contrast is used accurately to explain a difference.
~C. "The moods were not the same because one was hopeful and one was serious."#Incorrect. This is understandable, but it does not use the target academic word.
~D. "The texts mooded each other with contrast."#Incorrect. The academic word is not used correctly.
}

::ENG1_U01_L06_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG1.V.01] Passage: The teacher asked students to explain why the chart was useful. Question: Which response uses academic vocabulary naturally?{
~A. The chart was useful because it did things.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
~B. The chart organized because it was charted.#Incorrect. The word organized is not used clearly.
~C. The chart had useful information stuff.#Incorrect. Stuff is vague and not academic.
=D. The chart was useful because it organized the information clearly.#Correct. Organized is an academic word used naturally and accurately.
}

::ENG1_U01_L06_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG1.V.01] Passage: The student said the source was helpful because it gave facts from a school survey. Question: Which academic word best describes the facts from the survey?{
~A. decoration#Incorrect. Facts from a survey are not decoration.
=B. evidence#Correct. Evidence means information that supports an idea.
~C. rumor#Incorrect. A survey gives collected information, not a rumor.
~D. guess#Incorrect. Facts from a survey are not guesses.
}

